namespace SortingLab.Core.Words;

/// <summary>Пример текста на английском языке для сортировки слов.</summary>
public static class SampleText
{
    public const string English = """
        The library in our town stands at the end of Harbor Street, next to the old fish market. It is a small brick building with a green door and two tall windows that face the sea. On rainy days the reading room is full, because people come in to dry their coats and stay for the books.

        Mrs. Alder has worked there for thirty years. She knows where every book lives, and she knows which children read too fast and which read too slow. When a new box of books arrives, she sorts them on the long table by the window. First she puts the books in piles by subject: history, science, travel, stories for children. Then she sorts each pile by the name of the author, from A to Z. It takes her a whole afternoon, but she never seems to hurry.

        Last spring a boy named Tom asked her why she did not just put the books on the shelves in any order. She smiled and handed him a list of twenty titles. "Find these for me," she said. Tom found the first five quickly, because they were on the shelf in the right place. The sixth book had been left on a cart, and the cart was a mess. He spent ten minutes looking through it and found the book at the very bottom.

        "Now you see," said Mrs. Alder. "When things are in order, you can find them in a minute. When they are not, you waste your time and your patience."

        Tom thought about it for a while. The next week he came back with his little sister and offered to help with the new box. Mrs. Alder gave them each a pile. Tom sorted his books one by one, always looking for the right place for the next book in his hands. His sister had a different idea. She split her pile into two smaller piles, sorted each of them, and then put them together. They finished at almost the same time and argued all the way home about whose method was better.

        The library still has the green door and the two tall windows. If you visit on a rainy day, you may find Tom at the long table by the window, sorting a new box of books while the sea outside turns grey.
        """;
}
